column4:
Base: When n is 0, $\sum_{0}^{0}$(p*p!)=0; right side is(0+1)!-1=0;
Inductive hypothesis: $\sum_{0}^{p}$(p*p!)=(n+1)!-1 for n = 0,1,2,3,4...k-1 (for k >= 0);
Inductive step: $\sum_{0}^{k}$(k*k!)=(k+1)!-1 for k >= 0;

 For our base case, note that when 
let $\sum_{p=0}^{n}$ (p * p!) = (n + 1)! - 1, for all natural numbers n.
p! =  p + (p+1)....(p + p);
(n + 1)! = (n + 1)...... (n + 1 + n + 1);
then, p * p! =p * (p + (p+1)....(p + p));
(n + 1)! - 1 = (n + 1)...... (n + 1 + n + 1) - 1;
supose both of them are 0, the both of the value = 1;
that is what we need to show
